  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Barinov's wild room-service demonstration scares a guest, Mikhail threatens a lawsuit, Senya calls supplier Timur to buy new racing cockroaches, and Denis mistakenly attacks a motorcycle mechanic talking to Katya. Listen to the exchange.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ты что устроил? Нас же засудят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто-то не оценил твой отличный ход?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Очень странно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мы столько денег сегодня потеряли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Слушай, может, у Тимура закажем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Тимур, привет, это Сеня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты можешь нам тараканов достать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Да, мы тебе хорошо заплатим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Давай сразу пять штук.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебе говорил: не лезь!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ты вообще кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Отпусти его прямо сейчас!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты ему дала свой телефон?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты теперь доволен?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ты что устроил? Нас же засудят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did you do? They will sue us.' The '-l' ending on the verb meaning 'you staged' places the action in the masculine past tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Кто-то не оценил твой отличный ход?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did someone not appreciate your great move?' The meaning hinges on 'someone' working together with 'move' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Очень странно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Very strange.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'very' and 'strangely' in a few short words. Senya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мы столько денег сегодня потеряли.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We lost so much money today.' Senya brings out the contrast between 'we' and 'so many' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Слушай, может, у Тимура закажем?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, maybe we should order from Timur?' In Russian, the idea of 'listen' flows naturally into 'will order' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Тимур, привет, это Сеня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Timur, hi, this is Senya.' The meaning hinges on 'Timur' working together with 'Senya' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты можешь нам тараканов достать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can you get us some cockroaches?' Senya addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'to get'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Да, мы тебе хорошо заплатим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, we will pay you well.' The grammar packs 'yes' and 'we will pay' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Давай сразу пять штук.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Give us five of them right away.' Notice how Russian expresses 'come on' and 'pieces' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я тебе говорил: не лезь!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I told you: stay away!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'poke nose'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ты вообще кто такой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Who are you at all?' Mikhail addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'such'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Отпусти его прямо сейчас!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let go of him right now!' An emphatic spoken line centered on the verb meaning 'let go'. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты ему дала свой телефон?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you give him your phone number?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'you' before landing on 'phone'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты теперь доволен?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you satisfied now?' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'satisfied' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
